    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "Oh it's because in `react-instantsearch` we accept both function and class components through `React.JSXElementConstructor`, while in `instantsearch-ui-components` it's much more restrictive and only accepts function components that return a single `JSX.Element`.\r\n\r\nI think what Taylor did is good and can be simplified with : \r\n```jsx\r\nconst bannerComponent = (\r\n    BannerComponent === false ? () => null : BannerComponent\r\n  ) as HitsUiComponentProps<Hit<THit>>['bannerComponent'];\r\n```\r\nThat way we don't need to pass an undefined `banner`, we just pass a dummy component.\r\n\r\nLater down the line we'll have to review `instantsearch-ui-component` types because I don't think instantiating component wrappers inline in render like this is good as it makes the user's component remount each time, which might raise issues later if we want to push single object ID hits. (we could wrap them in `useMemo` though)",
    "commit_id": "1fdb88fbc8c7f0c1738ca41a99202aab6f99ca07",
    "created_at": "2024-04-30T08:33:07Z",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -65,10 +82,19 @@ export function Hits<THit extends BaseHit = BaseHit>({\n     </li>\n   );\n \n+  const banner = BannerComponent === false ? undefined : bannerData;\n+\n+  const bannerComponent =\n+    BannerComponent === false\n+      ? undefined\n+      : (BannerComponent as HitsUiComponentProps<Hit<THit>>['bannerComponent']);",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/algolia/instantsearch/pull/6170#discussion_r1584361559",
    "id": 1584361559,
    "in_reply_to_id": 1583884220,
